semantic_diff: >-
  Base: revision 13, digest sha256:0d9de42c61ad4bdffa6d6f73c2bf184f6fda059946b1a34513062f392d3c145d,
  verified intact with pave-init 2.6.2 record_revision.py (17 entries). Counts: 22
  nodes, 69 edges, 69 outcomes, 17 checks (one with changed text: batch_review_current),
  24 evidence definitions, 5 control nodes - all unchanged; roles, node roles,
  consumes and produces, required_evidence, allowed and forbidden effect lists,
  state.required and write_limits equal, proved by parsing both graphs and comparing
  the skeletons field by field. 7 hunks, 33 added lines against 12 removed; 2409
  lines become 2430 (ceiling 3100). No new node, outcome, edge, check, seat, model,
  effort or write limit. Three defects fixed by text at the nodes that own them, one
  refactor, four defects recorded with no change and the reason. Round-1 review
  (proposals/v14-graph-review-20260919T021821Z.md, PASS with 4 LOW) repaired in
  this same revision, every finding taken.

  (1) A DECLARED DOOR FOR THE USER'S OWN CHANGESET (D3). New work-item class at
  scope_next_increment, the fold item: minted at the round that first reads a user
  decision, in the user's own words, that a changeset the campaign did not design
  (the user's own branch or pull request against the campaign branch) lands on the
  campaign branch; selected ahead of plan items because every later item builds on
  the tip; its acceptance, defined there once, is the folded tip's diff against the
  pre-fold tip equal as a set of added and removed lines to the changeset's diff
  against its own base (values git produces, pave-spec 5.3.1) plus the whole-tree
  CPU-mode test pass on the folded tip; its review is the batch review of its landed
  commits, as for every increment. realize_increment realizes it (rebase onto the
  tip, conflicts resolved to the exact union, whole-tree pass, commits landed as
  written so the reviewer reads what the user wrote) and increment_passed lists its
  acceptance beside the other classes. batch_review_current counts a fold item's
  commits as one landed unit whatever their count, because they arrive together and
  nothing builds between them - the size call exists to catch a defect before
  dependents build on it, and a fold has none inside it; review_increment_batch's
  purpose points at that rule and states nothing twice. Before: the graph had no
  route for code the campaign did not design. PR 6 got a fresh adversarial reviewer
  before any landing, a rebase seat dispatched at prepare_pr (a node that assembles
  the fork PR package and never touches the branch), and a fold by the lead
  (LEAD-LOG 1964 to 1970, DECISIONS 1075); PR 7 is on the same hand-made path now
  (LEAD-LOG 2032: a reviewer seat at realize_increment, a node whose roles carry no
  reviewer). The landing path has no declared name for anything but a plan item -
  the lead still plans landing rows under record_changeset, a node revision 11
  removed (LEAD-LOG 1852, 1861). Why general: a user who develops on the fork beside
  the campaign will hand it changesets, and every such changeset moves the tip every
  later item and every measurement stands on, so the graph needs one door for it in
  every run. Why a work-item class and not a node or outcome: one implementer does
  the rebase and decides done from two git line sets (pave-spec 9.12); the class
  pattern exists at this node (revision 13 added the localization item the same
  way); a node would cost an outcome, an edge and a seat per fold. Why no pre-fold
  reviewer: the batch review is the declared review of every landed commit before
  the next scope round, and a second review of the same commits before the same
  harm is the gate pave-spec 4.11 excludes; the material findings of the PR 6
  review (F4, F5) are exactly what material_findings routes to scoping as repair
  items, which is where they went by hand as debts. Authority kept: the scope line
  "Merging PRs (human)" stands - the class text says the merge decision stays the
  user's and the campaign only lands commits on its own branch, a write
  realize_increment's allowed effects already grant; the user's recorded word is
  the trigger, so the campaign never pulls foreign code on its own. Cost per run:
  zero unless the user folds; then the two traversals every increment already pays
  (realize, batch review) in place of three hand-made seats.

  (2) A LOST RUN IS A DEFECT OF THE PROCEDURE'S FORM (D1). measure_candidate's
  procedure_defect_found now reads: a run exposes a defective implementation of a
  frozen comparator - it measured the wrong property, or its runner could not carry
  a run to its record while the host and the candidate stood (a transport bound to
  the workstation's session, a staging step); routed back to measurement
  preparation, where the smoke run covers the repaired form. Before: "a procedure
  that measured the wrong property", so attempt 29 - the M4 measure leg killed by
  the workstation's network loss through a foreground ssh pipe, host healthy,
  candidate serving, no number produced (LEAD-LOG 2008, defect class BU) - fit no
  declared outcome and the lead booked the nearest one with the mismatch recorded
  (run-state row 1623). After: the same route, now declared. Why this widening and
  not a new outcome: a procedure is its runner too (prepare_measurements
  smoke-verifies the set the runner belongs to), the repair the defect needs is a
  procedure revision through the same door, and a lost run with no procedure defect
  does not exist - a host fault is the node's lost-serving path and
  serving_exhausted, and a workstation fault that reaches the host only through a
  runner that depends on the workstation is, by that fact, the runner's defect. One
  clause, no edge. Cost per run: zero. Recorded outcomes: row 1623 took this edge
  under the old text; the new text admits it exactly, so no applied item's
  acceptance or route changes and the run continues on the successor with no
  catch-up duty (revision rule 7).

  (3) ONE CAMPAIGN, ONE CAMPAIGN LEASE (D4). prepare_host's lease rule gains one
  clause: a campaign lease asked for by a campaign that already holds one on that
  host is refused with the standing lease's reference, so one campaign never holds
  two. Before: the rule already said never re-request a lease the campaign holds
  (probe-only mode) and the queue tool did not check - a lane seat wrote a second
  campaign-kind grant for the one campaign (LEAD-LOG 1958, "Stray lease"), status
  then showed two, and a release by campaign would have released both. Enforcement
  level (pave-spec 9.14.1): mechanical, at the one writer of lease records, because
  the violation is a tool call a seat makes by omitting --job, is precisely
  detectable from the open leases the tool already reads under its lock, and the
  reminder rung (the existing prose) failed once; not stronger than needed - a
  refusal that names the standing lease costs the caller nothing and strands
  nothing. Release layer: hardware_queue.py cmd_grant refuses when --job is absent
  and an open campaign lease for that campaign names that host (status refused, the
  lease_id, exit 2) plus one test in tests/test_hardware_queue.py. Cost per run:
  zero.

  (4) CUT. review_increment_batch's purpose restated the batch size rule in full (3
  lines: 1 for a complex commit, up to 3 when low complexity); it now points at
  batch_review_current, which was already the mechanical authority and gains the
  low-complexity examples the node alone carried (config, tests, wiring, citation
  repairs) and the fold-unit count, so the rule lives once. Net growth: 21 lines -
  the fold class, defined once at scope_next_increment and pointed at from
  realize_increment, increment_passed and the batch size rule, and two clauses.

  (5) NO CHANGE, WITH THE EVIDENCE (D2, D5, the second half of D1, D6). D2, queue
  priority: the incident (LEAD-LOG 2003 to 2006) was one running 64-core CPU job
  under an open lease; the measurement asked for the whole host and waited 8
  minutes until that job's own release. No admission rule pre-empts a running
  lease, so a priority or reservation rule would have changed nothing here; the one
  thing the tool does not do - admit waiting requests in order, so a later short
  grant cannot slip ahead of a waiting whole-host request - was needed zero times
  in the incident, because the lane asked for no new lease. An admit-in-request-order
  rule earns its place when the lease records show a grant slipping ahead
  of a waiting one; none does, so this revision adds nothing and names the reading
  the next audit takes. D5, the measurement instance closed without an outcome
  (LEAD-LOG 1989): the instance for attempt 28 was opened before its entry edge
  fired (LEAD-LOG 1985 re-noded the measurer seat to measure_candidate at 14:23Z;
  measurements_ready_to_candidate_runs follows candidate_serving, and the attempt
  failed on its first request at 14:34Z), so it had no outcome to close by;
  measure_candidate is one instance per campaign (instance_per), and a second one
  per attempt is a lead routing act against the graph as written, not a gap in it.
  The M4 instance that stayed open through the breaker and the redesign is the
  declared shape: it closed by procedure_defect_found (row 1623) and re-entered
  through measurements_ready (row 1624). D1, second half (LEAD-LOG 2028): a
  diagnostic bundle complete while the gating bundle does not exist is working
  state inside the open instance; the bundle file is its record
  (measurement_artifacts, one file per event) and bundles_stable reads every
  declared bundle at once; an outcome for "some bundles done" would route nowhere
  and is the re-check pave-spec 4.11 excludes. D6, the write-report tool gaps
  (audit-findings.md, the instrument-gaps section): items 1, 2, 4, 5, 7 and 9 to 14
  are the tool's and the hook's (pave-init's run_write_report.py; the plugin's
  hooks.json --memory-dir flag and stop-guard.sh), not graph lines; they go on the
  release list in (8) as tool work, and the graph is unchanged by them.

  (6) USAGE EVIDENCE READ. No usage log exists (the run has had no terminal
  close). Run state since revision 13 applied at 2026-09-17T14:31:26Z (rows 1603 to
  1625, 0-based, 23 rows): realize_increment increment_passed 4;
  scope_next_increment increment_selected 4; review_increment_batch batch_sound 4
  of 4 and review_implementation ready_for_hardware 1 of 1 (33 of 114 and 91 of 226
  material over the run, per the revision 13 read - neither is a
